1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,417
Dev wants the six-pack
at two hundred rupees.

2
00:00:03,417 --> 00:00:05,290
We are premium, he says.

3
00:00:05,590 --> 00:00:06,914
Arjun wants eighty.

4
00:00:06,914 --> 00:00:08,486
Cheap sells, he says.

5
00:00:08,836 --> 00:00:11,178
Today it costs a hundred and twenty.

6
00:00:11,178 --> 00:00:12,401
So who is right?

7
00:00:12,401 --> 00:00:14,847
It depends on what the price must do.

8
00:00:15,497 --> 00:00:18,250
Laddoo Lab now has a
name that parents trust.

9
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:21,469
A six-pack costs a
hundred and twenty rupees.

10
00:00:21,469 --> 00:00:23,584
That is twenty rupees a laddoo.

11
00:00:23,934 --> 00:00:25,643
Zoya calls the team together.

12
00:00:25,643 --> 00:00:28,814
Before we pick a number,
she says, let's pick a goal.

13
00:00:29,464 --> 00:00:31,583
So what is a pricing objective?

14
00:00:31,883 --> 00:00:35,858
A pricing objective is what a
company wants its price to achieve.

15
00:00:36,208 --> 00:00:39,677
A pricing strategy is the
plan to reach that objective.

16
00:00:40,027 --> 00:00:41,845
Why does it matter so much?

17
00:00:41,845 --> 00:00:44,601
Product, place and
promotion all cost money.

18
00:00:44,601 --> 00:00:48,053
Price is the only part of
the mix that brings money in.

19
00:00:48,703 --> 00:00:51,816
Two famous strategies are
used for a new product.

20
00:00:51,816 --> 00:00:55,036
Zoya plays out both on
paper, for a new flavour.

21
00:00:55,386 --> 00:00:56,532
First, skimming.

22
00:00:56,532 --> 00:01:00,158
Start with a high price, for
keen buyers who want it first.

23
00:01:00,508 --> 00:01:03,202
A hundred and eighty rupees in week one.

24
00:01:03,202 --> 00:01:04,564
Only twenty buyers.

25
00:01:04,914 --> 00:01:09,207
Then the price steps down, to one
fifty, then one twenty, and more people

26
00:01:09,207 --> 00:01:10,107
join.

27
00:01:10,354 --> 00:01:11,685
Now, penetration.

28
00:01:11,685 --> 00:01:14,227
Start low, to win many buyers fast.

29
00:01:14,577 --> 00:01:16,693
A hundred rupees from day one.

30
00:01:16,693 --> 00:01:19,069
Sixty families try it in week one.

31
00:01:19,419 --> 00:01:22,369
By week four, ninety
families buy every week.

32
00:01:22,719 --> 00:01:24,930
Skimming earns more on each pack.

33
00:01:24,930 --> 00:01:27,377
Penetration wins more people, faster.

34
00:01:28,027 --> 00:01:29,752
Every price has a job.

35
00:01:29,752 --> 00:01:31,362
Choose the job first.

36
00:01:32,062 --> 00:01:36,194
Philip Kotler and Kevin Keller list
five common pricing objectives.

37
00:01:36,194 --> 00:01:38,634
Where do we see them at work today?

38
00:01:38,984 --> 00:01:39,884
Survival.

39
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:41,681
Cover costs and stay open.

40
00:01:41,681 --> 00:01:45,463
An airline cuts fares in a
slump to keep planes flying.

41
00:01:45,813 --> 00:01:47,407
Maximum current profit.

42
00:01:47,407 --> 00:01:49,173
Earn the most money now.

43
00:01:49,173 --> 00:01:52,765
A hotel near a stadium
charges the most on match day.

44
00:01:53,115 --> 00:01:54,701
Maximum market share.

45
00:01:54,701 --> 00:01:57,188
Price low to win the most buyers.

46
00:01:57,188 --> 00:02:00,660
A new delivery app launches
with very low prices.

47
00:02:01,010 --> 00:02:02,188
Market skimming.

48
00:02:02,188 --> 00:02:04,201
Start high, then come down.

49
00:02:04,201 --> 00:02:07,524
A phone maker cuts the
price of last year's model.

50
00:02:07,874 --> 00:02:09,670
Product quality leadership.

51
00:02:09,670 --> 00:02:12,162
A higher price for the best quality.

52
00:02:12,162 --> 00:02:15,118
A luxury hotel charges
more for top service.

53
00:02:15,768 --> 00:02:18,094
So which goal does Laddoo Lab choose?

54
00:02:18,444 --> 00:02:19,516
Not survival.

55
00:02:19,516 --> 00:02:21,460
The kitchen is doing well.

56
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:23,132
And not eighty rupees.

57
00:02:23,132 --> 00:02:25,076
Nani will not cut corners.

58
00:02:25,426 --> 00:02:26,941
Not two hundred either.

59
00:02:26,941 --> 00:02:29,784
Parents buy every week,
and must keep buying.

60
00:02:30,134 --> 00:02:33,058
Zoya picks quality
leadership, at a fair price.

61
00:02:33,058 --> 00:02:36,038
The six-pack stays at a
hundred and twenty rupees.

62
00:02:36,688 --> 00:02:38,718
But is pricing always fair?

63
00:02:38,718 --> 00:02:39,702
Not always.

64
00:02:40,052 --> 00:02:41,152
The good side.

65
00:02:41,152 --> 00:02:45,253
Clear prices help people choose,
and help a business plan ahead.

66
00:02:45,603 --> 00:02:46,784
The hard side.

67
00:02:46,784 --> 00:02:49,729
Early buyers feel
cheated when prices drop.

68
00:02:49,729 --> 00:02:52,141
And price wars hurt small sellers.

69
00:02:52,491 --> 00:02:57,031
Arjun asks, why not sell below cost
for a while, to beat the stall next

70
00:02:57,031 --> 00:02:57,931
door?

71
00:02:57,572 --> 00:03:00,015
Nani says, that is not how we win.

72
00:03:00,365 --> 00:03:05,428
In India, the Competition Act of 2002
bans predatory pricing by a dominant

73
00:03:05,428 --> 00:03:06,328
firm.

74
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:09,583
That means selling below
cost to push rivals out.

75
00:03:09,933 --> 00:03:10,833
The lesson?

76
00:03:10,793 --> 00:03:13,927
Give every price a clear
goal, and keep it fair.

77
00:03:14,577 --> 00:03:15,540
Quick check.

78
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:19,226
Zoya launches at a low price,
to win many buyers fast.

79
00:03:19,226 --> 00:03:20,855
Which strategy is this?

80
00:03:22,755 --> 00:03:23,746
Penetration!

81
00:03:23,746 --> 00:03:26,489
A low price opens the market quickly.

82
00:03:27,089 --> 00:03:28,098
So, remember.

83
00:03:28,398 --> 00:03:31,816
A pricing objective is what
the price must achieve.

84
00:03:31,816 --> 00:03:33,075
Decide it first.

85
00:03:33,425 --> 00:03:38,212
Five common goals: survival, profit,
market share, skimming and quality.

86
00:03:38,562 --> 00:03:41,038
Skimming starts high and comes down.

87
00:03:41,038 --> 00:03:43,565
Penetration starts low to win buyers.

88
00:03:44,165 --> 00:03:48,445
Next time, we'll explore cost-based,
competition-based and value-based

89
00:03:48,445 --> 00:03:49,345
pricing.
